  "strategy": "**Proposed Improvement:** \nWe introduce a **piecewise-distribution optimization** approach that combines focused gradient descent with global improvements via period doubling. \n\n**Implementation Plan:** \n\n1. **StepFunction Construction via Focused Gradient Descent**\n   - Define a base sequence of length 1600 using weighted exponential drops\n   - Score optimizable parameters (drop positions, converged rate) with a hybrid method:\n\n   ```python\n   def get_params(a):\n       \"\"\"Extract optimizable parameters from sequence\"\"\"\n       params = []\n       for i in range(len(a)):\n           if a[i] > 50:  # use local maxima to build parameterization\n               params.append(1/(i**0.5)*0.2)  # normalize factors\n           elif a[i] > 5:\n               params.append(1/(i**0.3))\n           else:\n               params.append(float(i))\n       normalize = lambda x: x / sum(params)**0.7\n       return params, normalize\n\n   def optimize_params(time_budget=600):\n       \"\"\"Parameter-based optimization using correlation coefficients\"\"\"\n       params = np.random.randn(32) + 3  # 32 parameters with safe initialization\n       score_history = []\n       max_iter = int(time_budget/10)\n       for i in range(max_iter):\n           p, normalize = get_params(params)\n           arr = np.exp(p) + np.exp(2*p)\n           arr = normalize(arr)\n           score = evaluate_sequence(arr)\n           score_history.append(score)\n           # Focus-based update: sharpen peak parameters when detected\n           if len(score_history)>2 and score < score_history[-1]:\n               p[score_history[-2]] += 0.2/np.sqrt(i)  # adaptive scaling\n       return params, score_history\n   ```\n\n2. **Period Doubling for Adaptive Expansion**\n   - Maintain candidate sequences in memory with dual membership\n   - For each parameter set, generate mirrored versions across power scales:\n\n   ```python\n   def expand_horizon(params_base, base_score):\n       \"\"\"Extend sequence length by power-of-2 scaling\"\"\"\n       amplifications = [2,4] + [8*i for i in range(2, int(time_budget/60)+1)]\n       best_score = base_score\n       best_params = params_base.copy()\n       for amp in amplifications:\n           params = deepcopy(params_base) * 0.2**amp\n           arr = build_sequence(params)\n           if len(arr)!= base_length * amp:\n               continue\n           iscore = evaluate_sequence(arr)\n           if iscore < best_score:\n               best_score = iscore\n               best_params = params\n       return best_params, best_score\n   ```\n\n3. **Hybrid Objective and Thermal Improvment**\n   - Track gradient moments for parameter selection\n   - Apply time-adaptive temperature adjustments:\n\n   ```python\n   def thermal_optimization(sequence, base_time):\n       \"\"\"Adaptive temperature-dependent gradient selection\"\"\"\n       grad_times = [0.1*i for i in range(10)]  # split gradient samples\n       best_score = float('inf')\n       dim_adjuster = quaternary_regularizer()\n       for t in grad_times:  \n           seq = sequence * dim_adjuster\n           validate_time = quaternary_validator(seq)\n           if validate_time > base_time*0.8:\n               continue  # skip unstable expansions\n           score = evaluate_sequence(seq)\n           if score < best_score:\n               best_score = score\n           if t<0.3*i and score is improving:\n               score = score * tune_factor(np.var(seq))\n       return seq, best_score\n   ```\n\n4. **Feasibility Enforcement**\n   - Use autoregressive validation with sparse gradient samples:\n\n   ```python\n   def validate_condition(seq, tol=0.5):\n       \"\"\"Check normalization and bounds\"\"\"\n       total = sum(seq)\n       log_info = logger()\n       if total < tol:\n           return stepwise_augmenter(seq)  # fabricate positive sum\n       average_val = np.mean(seq)\n       # Auto-normalization using gradient descent formatting\n       return { amount: log_info.gather-positive(sequence),\n                }\n   ```\n\n5. **Complexity Analysis**\n   - This hybrid approach reduces time complexity by focusing gradient updates\n   - independently of sequence length (O(n) savings via parameterization)\n\nThe core advantage is that we override the pure simplex projection with a  \nParameterized approach that better captures piecewise separation behavior  \nwhile maintaining the ability to extrapolate across different sequence lengths.  \nThe temperature adaptive unit ensures we don't lock into a local optimum  \ntoo quickly, providing better global exploration.",
